695	    '   - If a beat has no numbers and no listable facts, do NOT force a data visual — use kinetic_typography, quote_card, or dialogue_cards for that beat.',
696	    '6. TRANSITION DISCIPLINE (2–3 PER VIDEO, REUSED RHYTHMICALLY): pick at most three transition types for the whole reel (e.g. slide-left + fade, or wipe-left + flip-top + fade) and reuse them — random variety looks amateur. Direction-consistent: forward motion always enters from the same side. Calm beats use fade; app-like steps use slide; section changes use wipe; playful comparisons use flip-top.',
697	    '7. LIVING GRAPHICS (NO ANIMATED SLIDESHOW): every scene must MOVE for its whole duration — bars grow AND count, rings fill, lines draw, numbers tick, cards stagger in then breathe. A data visual whose cells are only static short text with nothing countable, fillable, or drawable is a FAILED scene — use kinetic_typography for text-only beats. Prefer visuals with built-in motion (comparison_matrix, radial_gauge, timeline_path, stat_spotlight, neural_flow) whenever the beat has anything measurable, sequential, or ranked.',
698	    '8. SELF-CHECK BEFORE RESPONDING (the reviewer rejects violations, so get it right first time): no adjacent duplicate visuals; every number in visualData appears verbatim in its beat; comparison_matrix ships ≥2 bars; metrics are ≤5 words; no headline identical to its beat; at most three transition types in the whole reel; every data visual has label AND value/detail filled from the beat — never empty strings.',
699	  ].join('\n');
